@topic backend.overview
title: The four backends
summary: interp, compile, cranelift-generic and cranelift implement the same semantics; they differ in speed, in where they run, and in the native-only operations only the native backends perform.
index: backends
alias: backends; which backend; backend comparison
keywords: interp, compile, cranelift, native, JIT, Tcl, speed, semantics
related: backend.interp backend.compile backend.cranelift backend.cranelift-generic backend.native-executable backend.native-only backend.io backend.performance task.choose-backend cli.options

== WHAT THEY ARE
    interp             the reference interpreter (the default): runs the
                       core IR the program's HIR lowers to; the executable
                       specification, deliberately simple and slow
    compile            compiles the program's HIR to Tcl procedures and
                       runs them: the same semantics, much faster than interp
    cranelift          compiles the HIR to native machine code with
                       Cranelift (JIT) after call-site specialization
    cranelift-generic  the same native backend without specialization
Select one with -backend NAME. `-emit-native-executable` uses the native
backend to build a standalone program (backend.native-executable).

== WHAT IS THE SAME
The language: values, errors, proofs and every compile-time diagnostic and
warning are decided before any backend runs, from the same analyzed HIR, so
a program is accepted or rejected identically everywhere. A program that
runs on two backends computes the same value and fails with the same error
code (the test suites check this across all four).

== WHAT DIFFERS
  * Speed: interp is the slowest by far (backend.performance).
  * Native-only operations: printing through io, raw system calls, machine
    addresses and C calls exist only on the native backends; interp and
    compile refuse them at run time with NATIVE-ONLY (backend.native-only,
    backend.io).
  * Availability: the native backends need the native executables (built in
    a checkout with cargo; packed in the single executable) and a supported
    platform (backend.platforms).
  * Input files: a .ir file (core IR text) runs on interp and compile only.

@topic backend.interp
title: interp: the reference interpreter
summary: The default backend: a tree-walking interpreter of the core IR the program lowers to; the executable specification of the semantics, and the slowest backend.
index: backends
alias: interp; interpreter; reference interpreter
keywords: interp, interpreter, reference, core IR, recursion limit
related: backend.overview backend.compile backend.performance

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend interp` (also the default). The front end lowers the analyzed HIR
to core IR, and core/evaluator.tcl walks it. It aims for clarity over
speed: it is the reference the other backends are tested against.

== THINGS TO KNOW
  * It is the slowest backend, often a hundred times slower than the native
    ones on search-heavy code; use `compile` or a native backend for real
    workloads, and keep tests that must also run here small.
  * Deep recursion works thousands of calls deep (the compiler raises Tcl's
    recursion limit for programs).
  * It refuses native-only operations (io printing, syscalls, C calls) with
    NATIVE-ONLY when they run.

@topic backend.compile
title: compile: the Tcl compiler backend
summary: Compiles the analyzed HIR to Tcl procedures and runs them: the same semantics as interp, many times faster, on any platform Tcl 9 runs on.
index: backends
alias: compile backend; tcl compiler
keywords: compile, Tcl, procedures, -code
related: backend.overview backend.interp backend.performance cli.options

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend compile`. compiler/compiler.tcl compiles the program's HIR once
into Tcl code (shown by -code) and runs it. With -backend compile, the
compiler also prints the inferred types of the program's top-level bindings.

== THINGS TO KNOW
Like interp it refuses native-only operations with NATIVE-ONLY. It is the
fastest backend that needs nothing but Tcl.

@topic backend.cranelift
title: cranelift: native code, specialized
summary: Compiles the program to machine code with Cranelift, specializing function instances by the argument kinds their call sites prove; prints the program's output as it runs.
index: backends
alias: cranelift; native backend; JIT; machine code
keywords: cranelift, native, JIT, specialization, NIR, CLIF, botlish-native
related: backend.overview backend.cranelift-generic backend.native-executable backend.native-only backend.platforms concept.specialization

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend cranelift`. native/lower.tcl lowers the analyzed HIR to NIR (the
native IR, shown by -emit-nir), the Rust driver botlish-native translates it
to Cranelift IR (-emit-clif) and runs the machine code against the native
runtime. Function instances are specialized by the argument kinds their
call sites prove (concept.specialization).

== THINGS TO KNOW
  * It performs the native-only operations: io printing, Linux system
    calls, C calls (backend.native-only). What the program prints appears
    on standard output and error as it runs, before the compiler's `value:`
    line.
  * It needs the native executables: in a checkout, `cargo build --release
    --manifest-path native/Cargo.toml` (otherwise NATIVE NOT-BUILT); the
    single executable packs them.
  * It is the JIT: the program is compiled into this process's memory and
    run there. The JIT is optional. The macOS application, Botlish.app,
    has none, and refuses -backend cranelift and cranelift-generic with
    NATIVE UNSUPPORTED jit. Natively, a program runs there as an
    executable (-aot-run, cli.aot-run), which every native build can do.
  * A construct the native lowering does not support fails with NATIVE
    UNSUPPORTED; native stack exhaustion is NATIVE LIMIT STACK.

@topic backend.cranelift-generic
title: cranelift-generic: native code, unspecialized
summary: The native backend without call-site specialization: one guarded, generic instance per function; the baseline the specialized backend is measured against.
index: backends
alias: cranelift-generic; generic native
keywords: generic, unspecialized, native, guards
related: backend.cranelift backend.overview

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend cranelift-generic`: the same pipeline as cranelift with
specialization off (BOTLISH_NATIVE_SPECIALIZE=0 has the same effect on
-emit-nir and -emit-clif). It runs the same programs with the same results;
it exists as a baseline and a cross-check, and on some programs compiles
faster.

@topic backend.native-executable
title: Standalone native executables
summary: -emit-native-executable FILE.bot compiles the program into a standalone executable (ELF, Mach-O or PE) that runs without Tcl, Rust or a checkout and prints what the program prints -- never its value.
index: backends
alias: native executable; standalone executable; emit-native-executable; compile to binary
keywords: executable, ELF, Mach-O, PE, exe, AOT, stub linker, rustc, emit, macOS, Windows, MSVC
related: backend.cranelift backend.single-executable backend.platforms cli.exit-status cli.run concept.argv

== WHAT IT IS
`-emit-native-executable game.bot` writes ./game (the input's name without
its extension; game.exe on Windows) and does not run the program. The
executable prints what the program prints and nothing else -- the
program's value is never printed -- and exits 0 when the program completes;
a run-time error goes to stderr with exit status 1. Its argv() is its real
process arguments. `botlish run game.bot ARG...` runs a program the same
way without building it (cli.run). It is an
ordinary command-line program of the host: an ELF executable linked
against glibc on Linux, a Mach-O arm64 executable linked against libSystem
(and signed ad hoc by the linker, as every arm64 executable is) on macOS, a
PE x86-64 console executable linked by Microsoft's link.exe, with the C
runtime linked into it, on Windows. It needs no source file, Botlish
checkout or compiler to run, only the system's own libraries (on Windows
no Visual C++ runtime either).

== REQUIREMENTS
Every function instance the program uses must be closed (fully known to the
ahead-of-time compiler); otherwise NATIVE AOT NOT-READY names the cause
(-aot-spec explains). A .ir file is rejected (NATIVE AOT INPUT): executables
are built from .bot, .hir or .hirx. Linking (BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER): in a
checkout the default linker uses rustc and the system C linker (rustc), on
Linux x86-64 and AArch64, on macOS AArch64 and on Windows x86-64 (MSVC's
toolchain: Visual Studio's C++ build tools and the Windows SDK, which rustc
finds itself); the `system` linker needs no rustc -- the host's C compiler
driver (cc; on Windows link.exe from a Developer Command Prompt) links the
program with the precompiled runtime archive (libbotlish_runtime.a, beside
the driver or BOTLISH_NATIVE_RUNTIME), on the same hosts; the `stub` linker
needs no toolchain and is Linux x86-64 only; `auto` (what the single
executable uses) is the system linker for a program that calls C and the
stub for any other. A program that calls C links with the rustc and the
system linker, its declared libraries passed to the platform linker, and
the stub linker never (NATIVE LINK). The
executable's machine code uses
the target's baseline instruction set, whichever native backend the
command line names: it is always the closed, specialized program.

== WHICH THREAD
An executable runs its program on a worker thread of its own by default;
-native-startup main-thread builds one that runs it on the process's main
thread, which a macOS graphical program needs (backend.native-startup).

@topic backend.native-startup
title: An executable's startup policy: worker or main thread
summary: -native-startup fixes the thread an executable runs its program on: a worker of its own (the default) or the process's main thread, with a 512 MiB stack (macOS AArch64 only).
index: backends
alias: native startup; startup policy; main thread; main-thread; run on the main thread; native-startup
keywords: -native-startup, worker, main-thread, main thread, Cocoa, AppKit, SDL3, LC_MAIN, stack_size, 512 MiB, pthread_main_np, macOS, graphical, window
related: backend.native-executable backend.platforms backend.macos-application cli.options error.native-unsupported error.native-startup error.native-limit-stack

== WHAT IT IS
A property of the executable, fixed when -emit-native-executable builds it
and recorded in it: never chosen by an environment variable when it runs,
by a library it links, a context or anything the program declares.

    worker        the default: the process's main thread starts a worker
                  thread, the program runs on it, and the main thread
                  waits for it; the worker's stack is
                  BOTLISH_NATIVE_STACK_BYTES (1 GiB by default)
    main-thread   the program runs on the process's main thread itself,
                  the thread that entered the executable's main, on its
                  own stack: 512 MiB, reserved by the link (Mach-O's
                  LC_MAIN, Apple ld's largest on arm64);
                  BOTLISH_NATIVE_STACK_BYTES does not apply

    botlish -native-startup main-thread -emit-native-executable game.bot

examples/games/icy-tower/build-macos.sh builds the Icy Tower game this way
with Botlish.app: one Mach-O with the pinned SDL3 linked into it as a
static archive, played in a Cocoa window with Metal.

== WHY
On macOS, AppKit -- and so SDL3's Cocoa video driver -- works on the
process's main thread only: on the worker, SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO) fails
with "No available video device". An executable built with main-thread
runs the same compiled program on the main thread, where a window, a Metal
renderer and its events work, from the program's own direct C calls; a
coroutine's stack is switched to on that same thread, so C calls made from
a coroutine are on the main thread too.

== WHAT IS THE SAME
The program's meaning, output, argv(), errors, exit statuses and the
driver protocol; the collector and its roots; coroutines; the stack
guard: exhausting the main thread's stack is NATIVE LIMIT STACK, status 1,
as on the worker (error.native-limit-stack), and a Rust panic is "native
executable panicked (NATIVE BUG)", status 1. What differs is the thread
and the stack's size: 512 MiB against the worker's 1 GiB, so the deepest
recursion a worker holds may not fit (it is then NATIVE LIMIT STACK).

== WHERE
main-thread is supported for macOS AArch64 executables only, through the
system linker (Botlish.app's) and the rustc linker alike. Every other
target refuses it before anything is written (NATIVE UNSUPPORTED startup):
Linux reports no guard below its main thread's stack, which the stack
guard needs; Windows' main thread has link.exe's 1 MiB unless a link
reserves more. The worker is supported everywhere. Nothing selects
main-thread automatically: linking SDL3 or Cocoa does not, and a program
that links them may run on the worker. -native-startup is an option of
-emit-native-executable only: a native run (-backend cranelift) keeps the
driver's worker, and a native library runs on its host's thread.

@topic backend.native-library
title: Native libraries: Botlish functions a C host calls
summary: `exported c fn` declares a C export; -emit-native-library NAME builds libNAME.a, libNAME.so, NAME.h and a manifest that any C host can link and call.
index: backends
alias: native library; library mode; exported c fn; emit-native-library; static library; shared library; C export; call Botlish from C
keywords: exported, export, library, .a, .so, header, manifest, instance, create, destroy, thread, C ABI, int64_t, owned buffer, Tcl extension, embedding
related: backend.native-executable concept.c-ffi cli.options error.export-signature error.export-placement error.export-name error.export-environment error.native-library error.native-limit-stack

== WHAT IT IS
A Botlish program can be a library a C program calls. Its exports are
declared in the source:

    error Rejected:
        code: int

    exported c fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Rejected:
        if n < 0:
            fail Rejected {code: n}
        n * 2

and `-emit-native-library NAME FILE.bot` builds (Linux glibc, x86_64 and
aarch64):

    libNAME.a            a static library: one object with the program and
                         a private copy of the runtime; only the library's
                         public C functions are global symbols
    libNAME.so           the same as a shared library
    NAME.h               the C interface
    NAME.manifest.json   the same interface as data, for tools

C calls `NAME_checked(instance, int64_t n, int64_t *result, NAME_error
**error)`. An exported c fn is an ordinary function to Botlish code and to
every backend; only a library makes a C symbol of it.

== THE C INTERFACE
  * Lifecycle: NAME_create makes an instance on the calling thread and runs
    the program's initialization (its modules' static initialization); the
    instance is called and destroyed (NAME_destroy) on that thread only,
    one call at a time. Instances share nothing; two libraries in one
    process share nothing but the C library.
  * Statuses: every call returns NAME_STATUS_OK, DECLARED (a declared
    Botlish error), RUNTIME, BOUNDARY (an argument or result C cannot
    carry), INVOCATION (a handle, thread or pointer the rules forbid) or
    INTERNAL. On failure the optional error out-parameter receives an error
    the caller owns: its identity (the canonical error name, e.g.
    "Rejected" or "module::Malformed", or a runtime error's code), its
    message, and its payload's int, bool, Float64 and str fields.
  * Types: int is int64_t (an Int result outside it is a BOUNDARY error,
    never wrapped), bool is bool, Float64 is double (NaN and the
    infinities are refused), str is UTF-8 -- a pointer and a length in, an
    owned NAME_bytes buffer out, released with NAME_bytes_release.

== THE EXPORT ROOT
An exported function is compiled for every argument its declared types
admit, never for what its Botlish callers happen to pass: Botlish calls of
it keep their own specialized code, and the export is a separate entry. A
library's entry file holds declarations only (NATIVE LIBRARY
LIBRARY-TOP-LEVEL), so no entry script runs when an instance is created.

== THREE DIFFERENT THINGS
  * A module's top-level functions are visible to every importer unless
    declared `private` (concept.private); there are no export lists.
    That is Botlish-to-Botlish reach, through the Botlish calling
    convention, and it makes no C symbol. `private` decides Botlish names
    only: a `private exported c fn` is still a C export.
  * `extern c fn` declares a C function Botlish calls (concept.c-ffi): a
    foreign import, with no body.
  * `exported c fn` gives a Botlish function a C entry point a host calls:
    a foreign export. It changes nothing for Botlish callers, which still
    call it directly; only -emit-native-library reads it. The explicit
    `-export FUNCTION` list (native::library -exports) selects top-level
    functions of the entry program the same way, checked by the same rules.

== LIMITATIONS
Botlish code runs on the caller's thread and stack. A library's Botlish
functions check their stack pointer, so recursion that would exhaust that
stack (or a coroutine's) fails the call with NATIVE LIMIT STACK
(error.native-limit-stack) and the instance answers the next call. No stack
guard is installed in a library: a C function or the runtime exhausting the
stack still ends the process. No callbacks, no Lists, structs or other
values at the boundary, no asynchronous calls; macOS and Windows libraries
are not made yet.

@topic backend.c
title: -emit-c: the program as freestanding C
summary: -emit-c writes a program as freestanding C11 for a target's own C toolchain; what C cannot implement (big Int arithmetic) becomes runtime-service calls a provider links in.
index: backends
alias: c backend; emit c; generate c; c code; freestanding c; embedded; microcontroller; c compile; runtime services; botlish_rt_v1
keywords: -emit-c, C11, freestanding, runtime service, provider, botlish_rt_v1.h, runtime.json, int64_t, link error, embedded
related: backend.overview backend.native-library cli.options error.c-unsupported error.c-runtime-limit concept.integers

== WHAT IT IS
`-emit-c FILE.bot` (or a .hir or .hirx file) writes, beside the input or in
`-out DIR`, and without running any C compiler:
    FILE.h              the program's types and its interface:
                        FILE_run(&value), FILE_status_code(status),
                        FILE_status_message(status), FILE_show(...)
    FILE.c              the program: one C function per specialized
                        function instance, no allocation, no I/O, no main
    FILE_display.c      optional: the program's value as text, the way
                        a run prints it
    FILE.runtime.json   the runtime services the generated C calls
    botlish_rt_v1.h     the runtime-service interface (only when needed)
FILE is the input's name as a C identifier; it prefixes every public name,
so several programs link into one image. The C needs only stdint.h,
stdbool.h and stddef.h: compile it with the target's compiler (-std=c11
-ffreestanding works), call FILE_run from the target's startup code.

== INTEGERS
An Int whose range the compiler proves fits int64_t is an int64_t, and its
+ - * < == mod run as C arithmetic that cannot overflow. Any other Int is a
botlish_rt_v1_int: the value inline while it fits int64_t, with portable
overflow checks, and a call of a runtime service (botlish_rt_v1_int_mul,
...) only when a result does not fit. A program that needs such a service
still compiles; it links only where a provider supplies the service, and
the linker names the missing symbol. Declaring integer domains (`type
Small = Int in 0..1000`) is how a program keeps its Ints in int64_t.

== WHAT IT COMPILES (milestone 1)
Functions, direct calls and self tail calls (loops), Int, Bool, unit,
enums, named and anonymous structs, a List literal as a value, if/elif,
bare loops, counted loops and folds, break, continue, return, declared
errors with fail and handlers, the run-time errors of mod and the shifts.
Anything else -- Strings, Lists of unknown length, closures, function
values, Float64, contexts, coroutines, C calls, error payloads -- is
rejected at compile time with C UNSUPPORTED (error.c-unsupported), never
compiled approximately.

== THINGS TO KNOW
  * The C toolchain itself may call memcpy, memset, memmove and memcmp
    (GCC and Clang require them of a freestanding environment), and on a
    32-bit target a 64-bit % may call a library routine: the target's, not
    Botlish's, obligations.
  * FILE.runtime.json lists every service the generated C names; an
    optimizing C compiler may prove a call unreachable and need fewer.
  * FILE_run is not reentrant when functions read top-level values (those
    are file-scope objects).

@topic backend.single-executable
title: The single-file compiler
summary: The packed `botlish` executable is this compiler, its command line, the standard library, Compilerpedia and both native executables in one Linux x86-64 file that needs only glibc.
index: backends
alias: single executable; botlish executable; packed compiler; zipkit
keywords: single, zipkit, zipfs, cache, install, glibc
related: backend.native-executable backend.platforms backend.macos-application cli.usage

== WHAT IT IS
A Tcl 9 zipkit (single/build.sh in a checkout): a static tclsh with the
compiler and main.tcl in an attached archive. It takes main.tcl's options,
runs every backend, builds standalone executables, and answers -help from
the packed Compilerpedia, with no source tree present. It links a program
that calls no C with the stub linker, which needs no toolchain, and one that
calls C (or declares a C library) with the system linker: the host's C
compiler driver (cc, with its linker) and the runtime archive the compiler
carries -- never Rust or a checkout (BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=auto).

== THINGS TO KNOW
On its first run that needs a native backend it copies its two native
executables into a cache directory ($BOTLISH_CACHE_DIR,
$XDG_CACHE_HOME/botlish or ~/.cache/botlish), and on its first run that may
link (-emit-native-executable, a native backend) its runtime archive too;
without a usable cache it uses a temporary one. Usage, -help and -version
need no installation. It is built for Linux x86-64 (on macOS the packaged
compiler is an application: backend.macos-application). A program that
calls C needs a C compiler on the machine that builds it (Debian, Ubuntu:
apt install gcc) and the libraries it declares; without one its link says
so (NATIVE LINK TOOLCHAIN). The executable it builds needs neither.

@topic backend.macos-application
title: The macOS application (Botlish.app)
summary: On Apple Silicon macOS the packaged compiler is Botlish.app, a relocatable application bundling Tcl, the compiler, the native backend and its runtime archive, signed ad hoc.
index: backends
alias: Botlish.app; macOS application; mac app; app bundle; Tcl.framework
keywords: macos, app, bundle, framework, zipfs, codesign, ad hoc, notarization, xcode-select, Library/Caches
related: backend.single-executable backend.platforms backend.native-executable error.native-link

== WHAT IT IS
single/macos/build.sh in a checkout builds Botlish.app and its download
archive, botlish-macos-aarch64.zip. The command is
Botlish.app/Contents/MacOS/botlish (a symbolic link to it on PATH works),
with every option and argument of the single executable. Inside:
Contents/MacOS/botlish (the launcher) and botlish-native (the native
driver), Contents/Frameworks/Tcl.framework (Tcl 9.0.1),
Contents/Resources/botlish.zip (the compiler, the standard library and
Compilerpedia, mounted read-only at //zipfs:/app) and
libbotlish_runtime.a (what executables are linked with). Move it anywhere;
it installs nothing and never writes into itself.

== THINGS TO KNOW
Analysis, -check, -help, the language server, interp, compile and native
runs of programs that call no C need nothing else installed. Every
executable is linked by the system linker (there is no stub on macOS), so
-emit-native-executable, and a native run of a program that calls C (Darwin
I/O does), need Apple's Command Line Tools (xcode-select --install) or
Xcode; never Rust. A graphical program's executable (SDL3's Cocoa driver,
AppKit) is built with -native-startup main-thread (backend.native-startup).
Diagnostics in library code name //zipfs:/app/...
paths; the language server copies the standard library to
~/Library/Caches/botlish (or $BOTLISH_CACHE_DIR) for an editor. The
application is ad-hoc signed, not notarized: a copy downloaded through a
browser is quarantined, and Gatekeeper refuses it until it is notarized.

@topic backend.native-only
title: Native-only operations
summary: Machine addresses, raw Linux system calls and C calls exist only on the native backends; interp and compile accept such programs but refuse the operation with NATIVE-ONLY when it runs.
index: backends rules
alias: native only; native-only operations; only on cranelift
keywords: NATIVE-ONLY, syscall, address, io, ffi, C, Tcl backends
related: backend.io backend.overview concept.c-ffi concept.direct-modules concept.context-traits

== WHAT THEY ARE
  * the address bridges std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes / from_mutable_bytes
    (and the aarch64 ones): a byte buffer's machine address;
  * the system-call transports std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall and
    std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall;
  * calls of C functions (`extern c fn`);
and so everything built on them: stdlib/linux.bot, std::linux::io, and
therefore io's printing through std::linux::io::LinuxIO. Each such
standard-library entry says so in its BACKENDS section (-help
std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall).

== WHY
The Tcl backends have no machine addresses and do not imitate the kernel:
pretending would make them disagree with the native backends about what a
program does. So they compile such a program like any other and stop at the
operation with a clear run-time error (CORE SEMANTIC NATIVE-ONLY).

== WHAT TO DO
Run the program with -backend cranelift (or cranelift-generic), or build an
executable. For a program that must run everywhere, keep the native-only
part at the edge: compute a value in portable code and print it only in a
native entry program, or return the result as the program's value, which
the compiler's development run prints on every backend (backend.io; an
executable and `botlish run` never print it).

@topic backend.io
title: Which forms of I/O work on which backend
summary: The program's value is printed on every backend; printing, reading stdin and the filesystem need a native backend or an executable; there is no exit-status API.
index: backends stdlib
alias: input output; io backends; printing on interp; output on every backend
keywords: io, print, print_line, stdout, stderr, value, native, interp
related: task.print-output backend.native-only stdlib.module.std::io stdlib.std::io::print_line stdlib.module.std::darwin::io stdlib.module.std::windows::io capability.input capability.files stdlib.module.std::io::fs capability.process-exit-status concept.program-value concept.context-traits cli.run

== OUTPUT, BY BACKEND
    the program's value          every backend: the compiler's development
                                 run prints it after "value:"; an
                                 executable and `botlish run` (cli.run)
                                 never print it
    std::io::print, print_line,  cranelift, cranelift-generic and native
    write_error(_line)           executables; interp and compile refuse the
                                 underlying system call (NATIVE-ONLY)
    std::linux::write,           the same: native only
    std::linux::io
    std::darwin::write,          the same: native only (C calls)
    std::darwin::io
    std::windows::write_file,    the same: native only (C calls)
    std::windows::io

io's operations need an installed context implementing std::io::IO; the
standard library provides three, one per platform: std::linux::io::LinuxIO
(`with context std::linux::io::create()`), which makes raw Linux system
calls; on macOS std::darwin::io::DarwinIO (`with context
std::darwin::io::create()`), which calls libSystem's write(2) through C; and
on Windows std::windows::io::WindowsIO (`with context
std::windows::io::create()`), which calls kernel32's GetStdHandle and
WriteFile through C (the text's UTF-8 bytes, exactly: no code page, no
newline translation) -- hence native only. The function that prints is the
same on all three; only the installed context differs.

== INPUT
    argv()                       every backend (concept.argv)
    std::linux::io::read(buffer)      standard input, raw bytes: cranelift,
    std::darwin::io::read(buffer)     cranelift-generic and native executables
                                 (capability.input)

There is no text or line input in io.

== FILES
    std::io::fs's operations,    cranelift, cranelift-generic and native
    std::linux::fs,              executables, with the platform's context
    std::darwin::fs,             installed; interp and compile refuse the
    std::windows::fs             underlying system call (NATIVE-ONLY)

The portable layer, std::io::fs, reads, writes and creates whole files and
lists, creates and removes directories through any platform's path type;
each platform module also streams through owned File handles
(capability.files).

== EXIT STATUS
A program cannot set its process exit status
(capability.process-exit-status).

@topic backend.platforms
title: Platforms and what runs where
summary: The Tcl backends need only Tcl 9; the native backend runs on Linux, macOS AArch64 and Windows x86-64, C calls too, variadic ones included; syscalls are Linux's; stub linking Linux x86-64's.
index: backends
alias: platforms; operating systems; aarch64; arm64; x86-64; supported platforms
keywords: Linux, x86-64, AArch64, arm64, macOS, Windows, MSVC, Tcl 9, coroutines, stub
related: backend.targets backend.overview backend.native-executable backend.native-startup backend.single-executable backend.macos-application

== WHAT IS TESTED
The repository's continuous integration runs every test on Linux x86-64 and
Linux AArch64 (the Tcl backends, the native backends, the golden suites)
and builds the single executable on Linux x86-64. A separate workflow runs
the native JIT, native coroutines, the native stack guard, standalone
executables, C calls and Darwin I/O on Apple Silicon macOS, and
another the native JIT, native coroutines, the native stack guard,
standalone executables, C calls (against libraries MSVC's cl builds),
Windows I/O, the collector under GC stress (its roots found in native
frames, checked against Windows' own unwinder), every Tcl test file on
the Tcl backends and every golden suite on all four, on Windows x86-64
with MSVC's toolchain. Nothing here is a promise about
other platforms.

== PER COMPONENT
    interp, compile          Tcl 9.0 or newer (Unicode-correct strings);
                             no machine-specific code
    cranelift(-generic)      the native executables (Rust, Cranelift);
                             Linux x86-64 and AArch64, macOS AArch64,
                             Windows x86-64 (MSVC's toolchain; MinGW's
                             windows-x86_64-gnu is no native target)
    native coroutines        Linux x86-64 and AArch64 and macOS AArch64
                             (hand-written stack switching), Windows
                             x86-64 (a Windows fiber per coroutine
                             stack), or, for a coroutine closed inside
                             one function, none (it is fused into that
                             function); the Tcl backends run coroutines
                             everywhere
    native stack overflow    NATIVE LIMIT STACK, for the thread's stack
                             and a coroutine's, on Linux x86-64 and
                             AArch64, macOS AArch64 and Windows x86-64
                             (error.native-limit-stack)
    Linux system calls, io   Linux; x86-64 through std::linux::abi::x86_64,
                             AArch64 through std::linux::abi::aarch64 (one
                             portable surface: linux, std::linux::io, io);
                             the linux namespaces compile for Linux
                             targets only (error.unsupported-platform)
    Darwin I/O, io           macOS AArch64: libSystem's read, write,
                             open, close and errno through C calls
                             (darwin, std::darwin::io, std::darwin::path; no raw
                             Darwin system call); the darwin namespaces
                             compile for macOS targets only
    Windows I/O, io          Windows x86-64: kernel32's GetStdHandle,
                             WriteFile and GetLastError through C calls
                             (windows, std::windows::io: standard output and
                             error only); the windows namespaces compile
                             for Windows targets only
    executables              Linux, glibc, ELF: rustc linker on x86-64
                             and AArch64, stub linker x86-64 only; macOS
                             AArch64, Mach-O: rustc linker; Windows
                             x86-64, PE (NAME.exe): rustc and link.exe
    main-thread startup      macOS AArch64 executables only
                             (-native-startup main-thread:
                             backend.native-startup); everywhere else
                             the worker, the default
    C calls (extern c fn)    Linux, glibc (GNU ld's link plan, the SysV
                             and AAPCS64 C ABIs), macOS AArch64 (Apple's
                             ld, Apple's arm64 C ABI) and Windows x86-64
                             (link.exe: /LIBPATH and NAME.lib, the Win64
                             C ABI, the static C runtime); variadic C
                             calls on all of them (Win64's through a
                             stub copying doubles into integer
                             registers); never with the stub linker
    single executable        Linux x86-64
    Botlish.app              macOS AArch64 (backend.macos-application)

@topic backend.targets
title: -target: the compilation target, an operating system and an architecture
summary: -target NAME picks the operating system and architecture a program is built for (C type sizes, modules, platforms); native code runs only for the host's own target.
index: backends
alias: target; -target; cross compile; C ABI target
keywords: target, linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos, macos-aarch64, windows, windows-x86_64-gnu, mingw, ffi, std::linux::sys, architecture, operating system, triple
related: backend.platforms concept.c-ffi cli.options error.unsupported-platform error.native-target error.hir-interchange-target

== WHAT IT DOES
The targets are linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos-x86_64, macos-aarch64,
windows-x86_64, windows-x86_64-gnu and windows-aarch64 (to the native
backend, triples: x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, ..., aarch64-apple-darwin); the
default is the host's. A target is an operating system and an architecture:
linux-aarch64 and macos-aarch64 share an instruction set, not a target. On
Windows the C toolchain's ABI family is part of the target too:
windows-x86_64 is MSVC's (x86_64-pc-windows-msvc), windows-x86_64-gnu
MinGW-w64's (x86_64-pc-windows-gnu), the target of the generated Win32
bindings (concept.c-bindgen-layouts).
The target decides:
  * the widths and signedness of std::ffi::c's C integer types
    (std::ffi::c::Long is 64 bits on Linux and macOS, 32 on Windows; plain
    std::ffi::c::Char is unsigned on Linux AArch64 only);
  * which file an architecture-specific module comes from (std::linux::sys
    is stdlib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot or stdlib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot);
  * whether a platform namespace is available: linux and everything
    under it exist for Linux targets only, darwin for macOS's and windows
    for Windows' (error.unsupported-platform);
  * the C calling convention of foreign calls (Apple's on macOS AArch64).
A HIR built with any of these records its target, and is never taken for
another's: a .hirx file of another target is refused
(error.hir-interchange-target), and the front-end cache keeps one entry
per target.

== WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
It is not cross-compilation to machine code: the native backends run and
emit code only for the host's own target, and refuse another target's
program (error.native-target). The Tcl backends run a program built for
any target. An unknown name is a usage error listing the targets.

@topic backend.hir-interchange
title: HIR interchange (.hirx) and the front-end cache
summary: -emit-hir-interchange writes a program's analyzed HIR as FILE.hirx, which any backend runs without the front end; the same format backs the opt-in front-end cache (BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE).
index: backends
alias: hirx; hir interchange; front-end cache; BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE
keywords: hirx, cache, interchange, fingerprint, front end
related: cli.options backend.overview

== WHAT IT IS
`-emit-hir-interchange game.bot` writes game.hirx and runs nothing;
`botlish game.hirx` (any backend) runs it with no parsing or analysis. A
.hirx file is valid only for the front end that wrote it: another one
rejects it with HIR INTERCHANGE COMPILER (the front end's identity is the
"front end" fingerprint -version prints). With BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE=DIR, a .bot
program's analyzed HIR is kept in DIR and reused while the program, its
modules and the front end are unchanged.

@topic backend.performance
title: How fast each backend is
summary: interp is the reference and the slowest; compile is many times faster; the native backends are fastest at run time but pay a fixed compilation cost per run.
index: backends
alias: performance; speed; slow interpreter; which backend is fastest
keywords: speed, slow, fast, benchmark, compile time
related: backend.overview task.choose-backend backend.native-executable

== ROUGH ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE
Measured on a 15-puzzle solver's IDA* search (an application study): interp
about 2.5 minutes, compile about 5 seconds, the native backends about 1.3 to
1.5 seconds -- most of which was fixed front-end and JIT cost; a standalone
executable ran the same workload's search in about 2 seconds of a longer
benchmark. Front-end time (parsing and analysis) is the same for every
backend and dominates short programs: a 600-line test suite took several
seconds to compile. These are observations, not guarantees.

== WHAT TO DO
Develop and test on the native backends or compile; keep inputs of tests
that must also pass on interp small; build an executable for repeated runs
(it pays no compilation per run).

@topic cli.usage
title: Invoking the compiler
summary: botlish [OPTION...] FILE... compiles and runs each file; with no arguments it prints the usage summary; -help opens Compilerpedia; -version prints the build identity.
index: backends
alias: usage; how to run; run a program; command line; cli
keywords: main.tcl, botlish, invoke, file, .bot, .hir, .hirx, .ir
related: cli.options cli.output cli.exit-status cli.packages backend.overview task.choose-backend backend.single-executable

== FORMS
    botlish FILE.bot                run the program on interp
    botlish -backend cranelift FILE.bot
    botlish -emit-native-executable FILE.bot     build ./FILE
    botlish run FILE.bot [ARG...]   run it as a command: its own output
                                    only, its arguments (cli.run)
    botlish -check FILE.bot         report its diagnostics, run nothing
    botlish -lsp                    the language server, for an editor
    botlish -help [QUERY...]        Compilerpedia
    botlish -version                the build identity
    botlish                         the usage summary (exit 0)
    botlish add|install|update|remove|verify|package ...
                                    the package manager (cli.packages)
In a repository checkout the compiler is `tclsh9.0 main.tcl` with the same
arguments. Running it with no arguments only prints usage: it runs no
program. The usage summary names the primer first (`-help primer`,
task.first-program), and `-help` alone opens with the primer's table.

== INPUT FILES
    FILE.bot    Botlish source; its imports name standard namespaces and
                the .bot files beside it (concept.modules)
    FILE.hir    HIR text
    FILE.hirx   HIR interchange (backend.hir-interchange)
    FILE.ir     core IR text: interp and compile only
Several files are run one after another, each with its own "== FILE"
header. A first argument that is a package command's word (add, install,
update, remove, verify, package, native) is that command, never an input
file; a first argument `run` is the run form (cli.run).

@topic cli.options
title: Every command-line option
summary: The options of botlish (tclsh9.0 main.tcl): backend and warning selection, program arguments, building, checking, the language server, inspection output, and the reference.
index: backends
alias: options; flags of the command line; command-line options
keywords: -backend, -warnings, -target, -L, -F, -runtime-path, -argv, -argv-hex, -argv-none, -aot-run, -aot-keep, --, -emit-native-executable, -native-startup, -emit-hir-interchange, -emit-native-library, -export, -out, -emit-c, -check, -color, -excerpts, -lsp, -lsp-scheduler, -lsp-worker, -code, -hir, -ast, -aot, -aot-data, -aot-spec, -contexts, -direct, -traits, -emit-nir, -emit-clif, -help, -version
related: cli.usage cli.output cli.exit-status cli.lsp cli.run cli.aot-run lint.policy backend.targets backend.hir-interchange backend.native-executable backend.native-startup backend.native-library backend.c concept.argv

== RUNNING
    -backend NAME     interp (default), compile, cranelift,
                      cranelift-generic (backend.overview)
    -warnings MODE    default (print warnings, run), off (no warning
                      pass), error (reject a program that has a warning)
                      (lint.policy)
    -target NAME      the target (operating system and architecture) a
                      .bot program is compiled, or a .hirx file read, for:
                      linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos-x86_64,
                      macos-aarch64, windows-x86_64, windows-x86_64-gnu,
                      windows-aarch64; default the host's
                      (backend.targets)
    -L DIR            add DIR to the system linker's library search path,
                      for a program that calls C, and to where a library
                      declared `static` finds its archive (concept.c-ffi,
                      concept.native-link-plan)
    -F DIR            add DIR to Apple's ld's framework search path (macOS),
                      for a program that declares `extern c framework`
                      (concept.native-link-plan)
    -runtime-path PATH  record PATH in an executable -emit-native-executable
                      links as a directory its loader searches for shared
                      libraries: an ELF RUNPATH, a Mach-O LC_RPATH
                      ($ORIGIN/lib, @executable_path/lib); refused on
                      Windows, whose loader searches the executable's own
                      directory (cli.packages: native deploy)
    -aot-run          build the one input as the executable
                      -emit-native-executable links, in a private
                      directory, and run it as a command: the words after
                      `--` its arguments, its output and exit status its
                      own; nothing is left behind (cli.aot-run). It takes
                      only -warnings, -target (the host's), -L, -F,
                      -native-startup and -aot-keep
    -aot-keep         keep -aot-run's build directory (the executable and
                      its NIR) and name it on stderr
    --                ends -aot-run's options: every word after it is the
                      program's argument

== PROGRAM ARGUMENTS (argv())
    -argv TEXT        append one argument (its UTF-8 bytes); the first one
                      given is argument zero
    -argv-hex HEX     append one argument given as the hex of its raw bytes
                      (so invalid UTF-8 is expressible; "" is an empty one)
    -argv-none        the empty argument vector
Without any of them argv() is the one-element default ["botlish-runner"],
never the compiler's own arguments. An executable ignores them: it reads its
own process arguments.

== BUILDING (nothing runs)
    -emit-native-executable   a standalone executable beside each input
                              (backend.native-executable)
    -native-startup POLICY    the executable's startup policy: worker (the
                              default: a worker thread of its own) or
                              main-thread (the process's main thread, a
                              512 MiB stack; macOS AArch64 only), fixed
                              into the executable (backend.native-startup);
                              an option of -emit-native-executable and
                              -aot-run only
    -emit-hir-interchange     each input's analyzed HIR as FILE.hirx
                              (backend.hir-interchange)
    -emit-native-library NAME the native library NAME from the one input:
                              libNAME.a, libNAME.so, NAME.h and
                              NAME.manifest.json (backend.native-library)
    -export FUNCTION          export the entry program's top-level FUNCTION
                              (repeatable; without it the library exports
                              the program's `exported c fn`s)
    -out DIR                  where -emit-native-library or -emit-c writes
                              (default: the input's directory)
    -emit-c                   each input as freestanding C11: FILE.c,
                              FILE.h, FILE_display.c, FILE.runtime.json
                              (the runtime services it needs) and, when
                              it needs one, botlish_rt_v1.h; no C compiler
                              runs (backend.c)

== CHECKING (nothing runs, nothing is built or written)
    -check            analyze each .bot input and report what compiling it
                      reports: the same error lines (and help lines), its
                      warnings on stderr, "ok" when it has no error; exit
                      status 1 when an input has one. Past a syntax error
                      it also reports the semantic errors the malformed
                      source cannot affect, which a run does not
                      (error.surface-syntax). Only -warnings and
                      -target (they decide the analysis), -color and
                      -excerpts combine with it; any other option, or an
                      input that is not a .bot file, is a malformed
                      invocation.

== DIAGNOSTICS (how printed errors look; cli.output)
    -color WHEN       color the printed diagnostics: the "error:",
                      "note:" and "help:" labels, the location, the code,
                      and the excerpt's source highlighted as an editor
                      would. WHEN is auto (the default: on a terminal,
                      unless NO_COLOR is set or TERM is dumb; never on
                      Windows, whose console needs -color always), always
                      or never
    -excerpts WHEN    after each error, the source lines it names,
                      underlined where it is ("^"). auto (the default: on
                      a terminal), always or never
Neither changes a diagnostic, its code or its order: output to a pipe or a
file is the plain format below, unless always is asked for. Both combine
with every run, `run` and -check.

== LANGUAGE SERVER (cli.lsp; no input file)
    -lsp              run the language server: the Language Server
                      Protocol on stdin and stdout, for an editor. It
                      analyzes what the editor opens exactly as -check
                      does, runs and builds nothing, and takes only
                      -warnings, -target and -lsp-scheduler.
    -lsp-scheduler NAME   where -lsp analyzes: worker (the default: a
                      persistent worker process, so the server keeps
                      answering while an analysis runs) or sync (in the
                      server's own process, the reference design)
    -lsp-worker       internal: the analysis worker -lsp starts itself
                      (a private protocol on stdin and stdout); not for
                      direct use

== INSPECTING (printed before the program runs)
    -code             the Tcl code the compile backend generates
    -hir              the program's semantic HIR
    -ast              a .bot file's surface syntax tree
    -aot              every function's closed-AOT readiness
    -aot-data         the same analysis as data
    -aot-spec         the AOT report of every specialized function instance
    -contexts         the context structs, installations and every
                      function's context requirements, with the chain that
                      explains each
    -direct           each module's directness (concept.direct-modules)
    -traits           traits, conformances and specializations
    -emit-nir         the native IR (NIR) of the program
    -emit-clif        the Cranelift IR of every function

== REFERENCE
    -help [QUERY...]  Compilerpedia: alone, its indices; with a query, the
                      topic or the matching topics. Every argument after
                      -help is part of the query. --help and -h are the same.
    -version          the build identity (--version is the same)

== ENVIRONMENT
    BOTLISH_WARNINGS=default|off|error   the warning mode of a compilation
                                         given no -warnings
    BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE=DIR                the front-end cache
    BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=rustc|stub     how executables are linked
    BOTLISH_LSP_STATS=FILE               -lsp writes its measurements to
                                         FILE when it exits
    NO_COLOR=anything                    -color auto colors nothing
    TERM=dumb                            -color auto colors nothing

== MALFORMED INVOCATIONS
An unknown option, an option without its argument (`-backend` last), an
unknown backend, mode or target, a malformed -argv-hex, an unknown
-native-startup policy, -native-startup without -emit-native-executable
or given twice with different policies, -check beside an
option it does not take or with an input that is not Botlish source, -lsp
beside an option it does not take or with an input file, an unknown
-lsp-scheduler, or options without an input file print a message on stderr
and exit with status 2; nothing is compiled.

@topic cli.output
title: What a run prints
summary: Per file: "== FILE (BACKEND)", the program's output (native backends), "   value: V"; or "   error:" lines, each with a "   help:" line naming its topic; warnings go to stderr.
index: backends
alias: output format; value line; what main prints
keywords: value, ==, error, help, warning, stderr, stdout, refinements
related: cli.usage cli.exit-status cli.run concept.program-value backend.io lint.policy

== A RUN
    == game.bot (cranelift)
    ...what the program printed (native backends)...
       value: 42
The value is the program's last expression, shown as a literal ("text",
[1, 2], {a: 1}, enum cases by name). With -backend compile the inferred
types of top-level bindings follow ("   type:  name : T"). A Block value
(a function) is followed by the refinements visible in its environment.

This is the development view of a run, on every backend. `botlish run
FILE.bot` prints none of it -- no header, no value -- only what the program
writes (cli.run).

== A REJECTED PROGRAM
    == broken.bot (interp)
       error: broken.bot:3:5: unbound name "x" (CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND)
       help: botlish -help error.unbound
Every independent diagnostic gets its own line; each is followed by a help
line naming the Compilerpedia topic of its rule or code, once per topic
per file.

On a terminal (or with -excerpts always) each error is followed by the
source lines it names, underlined where it is, and the line is colored
(or with -color always; cli.options, DIAGNOSTICS):
       error: broken.bot:3:5: unbound name "x" (CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND)
         |
       3 |     x + 1
         |     ^
       help: botlish -help error.unbound
In a pipe or a file the output is exactly the plain form above.

== WARNINGS
Printed on stderr as they are found, `FILE:LINE:COL: warning: MESSAGE
(CODE)` with `note:` lines for related locations, then one `help:` line per
distinct code. Standard output stays the program's.

== A CHECK
    == game.bot (check)
       ok
With -check (cli.options) the header names no backend and nothing runs: a
program without an error gets "ok"; a rejected one the same "error:" and
"help:" lines a run prints, and its warnings go to stderr as a run's do.
One difference: a program with a syntax error gets its syntax errors and
then the independent semantic errors of the code around them, where a run
stops at the syntax errors (error.surface-syntax, "Checking around a
syntax error").

@topic cli.exit-status
title: Exit statuses of the compiler and of executables
summary: botlish exits 0 when every input compiled and ran, 1 when any failed, 2 for a malformed invocation; -help exits 1 when nothing matches; an executable exits 0 or, after a run-time error, nonzero.
index: backends
alias: exit code; exit codes; compiler exit status
keywords: exit, status, 0, 1, 2, failure
related: cli.usage cli.packages cli.run capability.process-exit-status backend.native-executable

== THE COMPILER
    0   every input compiled and ran (or was built, or checked without an
        error), or usage, -help with a match, -version
    1   some input was rejected or failed at run time; or -help found no
        topic (near matches on stderr)
    2   a malformed invocation (stderr says what)
With -lsp: 0 when the editor ended the session in order (shutdown, then
exit), 1 otherwise (exit without shutdown, the end of its input, input that
cannot be read as protocol messages). The package commands (cli.packages):
0 on success, 1 when the operation failed (an "error:" line on stderr) or
verify found a problem, 2 for a malformed invocation.

== RUN AS A COMMAND (cli.run)
    0   the program completed
    1   the program was rejected (its diagnostics on stderr) or failed at
        run time (the error on stderr)
    2   a malformed invocation

== A STANDALONE EXECUTABLE
0 when the program completes (it prints nothing of its own); 1 after a
run-time error (on stderr). The program cannot choose its own exit status
(capability.process-exit-status).

@topic cli.run
title: Running a program as a command: botlish run
summary: botlish run FILE.bot ARG... runs a program as its executable would: only what the program writes (no header, no value), the words after FILE as its arguments, status 0 or 1.
index: backends
alias: run; botlish run; run a program as a command; utility; unix tool; run with arguments; no value line; quiet run
keywords: run, command, arguments, argv, stdin, stdout, value, status, utility, pipe
related: cli.usage cli.output cli.exit-status cli.aot-run backend.native-executable concept.argv concept.program-value task.print-output

== THE FORM
    botlish run [OPTION...] FILE.bot [ARG...]
    tclsh9.0 main.tcl run tool.bot -n 3 < input > output
Runs FILE as an executable built from it runs: its standard output and
error are the program's own -- no "== FILE" header, no "value:" line -- so
it can sit in a pipe. Every word after FILE is one of the program's
arguments, an option-like one too: argv() is FILE as typed, then the ARGs.
Standard input is the program's (std::linux::io::read).

== OPTIONS
Before FILE: -backend NAME (default cranelift: the program runs natively,
as its executable would; where the native backend has no JIT, as the
macOS application's has none, natively as an executable: cli.aot-run),
-warnings MODE, -target NAME, -L DIR, -F DIR, -color WHEN and -excerpts
WHEN (how a rejection's diagnostics look on stderr). Any other option
(inspection, building, -check, -argv) is a usage error.
The Tcl backends run it too, but cannot print (backend.io).

== WHAT IT PRINTS AND RETURNS
    the program completes         status 0; nothing printed of its own --
                                  the program's value is not printed
    the program is rejected       its diagnostics on stderr, each after
                                  "FILE: ", with their help lines; status 1
    it fails at run time          the error on stderr, "MESSAGE (CODE)", as
                                  an executable writes it; status 1
The program cannot choose its exit status (capability.process-exit-status).
To see a program's value, run it the development way (botlish FILE.bot,
cli.output).

@topic cli.aot-run
title: Building and running a program as a native executable: -aot-run
summary: botlish -aot-run FILE.bot -- ARG... builds the program as a standalone executable in a private directory and runs it as a command: its output, arguments and exit status its own, no JIT needed.
index: backends
alias: aot run; compile and run; build and run; run natively without the JIT; native development cycle; cargo run; go run
keywords: -aot-run, -aot-keep, --, executable, AOT, ahead of time, status, signal, exit code, temporary, private directory, main-thread, JIT
related: cli.run cli.options cli.exit-status backend.native-executable backend.native-startup backend.cranelift concept.argv error.native-unsupported error.native-link

== THE FORM
    botlish -aot-run [OPTION...] FILE.bot [-- ARG...]
    tclsh9.0 main.tcl -aot-run tool.bot -- -n 3 < input > output
    botlish -aot-run -native-startup main-thread game.bot
Builds FILE as `-emit-native-executable` builds it: the same compilation,
link plan, linker and startup policy. The executable goes in a new private
directory under the system's temporary directory, is run, and the
directory is removed when the program has ended. Nothing runs in the
compiler's own process, so no JIT is needed.

== THE PROGRAM'S PROCESS
  * Arguments: argv() is FILE as typed, then every word after `--`,
    exactly as given: spaces, an empty word, a word that looks like an
    option, another `--`.
  * Standard input, output and error are the program's, unchanged. The
    compiler writes nothing on standard output, and on standard error only
    its own diagnostics, each after "FILE: ".
  * The working directory and the environment are the user's.
  * The exit status is the program's own: 0 when it completes, 1 when it
    fails at run time (its error on stderr, as an executable reports it),
    the status a C `exit` gave. When signal N ends it the status is 128+N,
    as a POSIX shell's `$?` says, and a line on stderr names the signal
    (none for SIGINT and SIGPIPE).
  * Interrupted: Ctrl-C reaches the program as it would from a shell, and
    the build directory is removed. If the compiler's process is killed,
    the program is ended too: it never outlives the command that ran it.

== WHAT NEVER RUNS
A program that is rejected, is not AOT-ready, or does not link is not run:
its diagnostics on stderr, status 1. Every run builds anew, so a failed
build never runs an earlier executable of the same file. A signal that
comes before the program starts stops the run there.

== OPTIONS
-warnings, -target (only the host's: the program runs here), -L, -F,
-native-startup and -aot-keep. -aot-keep keeps the build directory, with
the executable and the program's NIR, and names it on stderr. Any other
option is a malformed invocation (status 2), as is a second input file or
`--` without -aot-run.

== AND `run`
`botlish run FILE ARG...` runs a program as a command too, on the JIT
where the native backend has one. Where it has none (the macOS
application), `run`'s native default runs the program as -aot-run does.
-aot-run behaves the same in every native build. A program that finds files
relative to its own executable sees the private build directory: build it
with -emit-native-executable for that.

@topic cli.packages
title: The package manager: botlish.json, .botpkg artifacts, add and install
summary: botlish add, install, update, remove and verify manage a project's packages -- one shared dependency graph -- from local .botpkg artifacts and botlish.json. Nothing is downloaded or run.
index: backends
alias: package manager; botlish.json; .botpkg; botlish add; botlish install; package artifact; lock file; dependency versions
keywords: add, install, update, remove, verify, pack, inspect, graph, package graph, shared dependency, DEPENDENCY-CONFLICT, held, native, plan, deploy, botlish.json, lock, request, repository, artifact, editable, sha256, .bot_modules, targets, target view, .targets, bundled, system library, origins, -target, -drop-target, native add, native require, native audit, package stage, package compare, provenance, evidence, runtime, run-time dependency, license, notices, schema 3, authoring
related: rule.package-modules concept.modules concept.native-link-plan cli.usage cli.exit-status backend.targets

== THE COMMANDS
    botlish add NAME|FILE.botpkg|DIR [-request R] [-replace]
    botlish install
    botlish update [NAME...]
    botlish remove NAME...
    botlish verify
    botlish package pack [DIR] [-out DIR] [-force] [-evidence FILE]
    botlish package inspect FILE.botpkg [-target T] [-provenance FILE]
    botlish package graph [-project DIR] [-repository DIR]...
    botlish native inspect FILE [-target T]
    botlish native plan [-target T] [-include]
    botlish native deploy -target T -out DIR [-force]
and, for a package's author (AUTHORING A NATIVE PACKAGE, below):
    botlish native add FILE -target T -name N [-symbol S]... [-license EXPR]
    botlish native require NAME -target T -origin system [-identity I]
    botlish native require NAME -target T -runtime always|capability|optional -purpose TEXT
    botlish package stage [DIR] -target T -modules|-headers|-files SRC [-as PATH]
    botlish native audit [DIR] [-target T]
    botlish package compare A.botpkg B.botpkg
(tclsh9.0 main.tcl add ... in a checkout.) add, install, update, remove
and verify take -project DIR (the project: the directory of its
botlish.json; default the current directory) and -repository DIR (a local
repository of NAME-VERSION.botpkg files; any number, never searched
implicitly); the first four also -force (replace installed packages that
were changed), -recover (roll back an interrupted operation), -target T
(prepare the target view of T) and -drop-target T (remove it); verify
-target T verifies that view's native dependencies again.

== WHAT THEY DO
  * add NAME installs the newest version a repository holds (or the newest
    -request accepts) -- or, when a package of the graph already locks
    NAME, the version it locks -- writes the request (MAJOR.MINOR by
    default) and the exact lock to botlish.json, and installs the package's
    own locked dependencies in the application's graph (ONE GRAPH, below).
    add FILE.botpkg records the archive and its SHA-256 as the dependency's
    source; add DIR (a path with "/") links an editable directory, which
    has no lock.
  * install recreates exactly what botlish.json locks, from the
    repositories; it never changes a lock. update moves locks to the newest
    version their requests accept (1.2 never becomes 1.3), and holds a
    dependency some package of the graph locks at that version, saying so;
    remove deletes a dependency, and the packages nothing else uses any
    more.
  * package graph shows the graph an install makes, without writing
    anything: every package once, its version, who uses it, who locks it,
    whether it is installed so, a path to it; or the conflict.
  * verify compares installed files with the installation record and,
    where a repository holds them, with the artifacts' bytes.
  * package pack makes a deterministic NAME-VERSION.botpkg from a package
    directory whose botlish.json has a name, a version and a lock for
    every dependency, checking a target-aware package's layout and every
    bundled native file first.

== ONE GRAPH
A package declares its dependencies and locks each to the release its
author built and tested it with; a package's dependencies are never its
consumer's to install. The application has one dependency graph: every
package in it once, at the one version every lock of it names, installed
at .bot_modules/NAME and shared by every package that depends on it
(.bot_modules/.graph says which uses which: rule.package-modules). Two
independently written libraries that both need event_loop_linux get one
instance of it, so the driver contexts the application installs once are
the ones both require, and its native archive is linked once. Locks that
disagree -- two packages locking two releases of one package, or the
application's lock and a package's -- are a DEPENDENCY-CONFLICT naming
every lock and the path to the package that holds it: no lock is ever
moved, relaxed or overridden to make the graph agree, and no second
instance is made. There are no peer dependencies: a library declares what
it uses like any dependency. An editable directory satisfies no package's
lock; two different files claiming one release are a CONFLICT; a package
that depends on itself through others is a CYCLE.

== TARGETS AND NATIVE DEPENDENCIES
A botlish.json of schema 2 may name the targets a package supports
(`targets`): for each, a directory of target-specific files (generated
bindings, native archives, shared libraries) and its native libraries --
bundled (a file of the package) or system (the target's own installation),
static or shared, with the facts the manager checks. One release (one name
and version, one SHA-256) holds every target it names. When an installed
package is target-aware, the manager derives a target view per prepared
target, .bot_modules/.targets/TARGET: every package with that target's
files at their module paths, and the bundled libraries in its .lib. The
compiler reads the view of the target it compiles for (rule.package-modules)
and links that .lib without -L; a native backend's run finds its shared
libraries there. Preparing another target never touches a view already
made; a package that does not name a target is UNSUPPORTED for it.
  * native inspect reads a native file (ELF, ar, Mach-O, PE/COFF) without
    running it; native plan shows a view's native closure -- what links,
    from where, verified how, and what must be deployed (-include prints
    the packages' public header directories as -I options); native deploy
    places a target's bundled shared libraries in DIR/lib (Linux, macOS)
    or DIR (Windows), as a transaction recorded in DIR, for an executable
    linked with -runtime-path '$ORIGIN/lib' (cli.options).
  * A system library is verified only on its own target's host, by the
    system's metadata (its dpkg package or a pkg-config record), never by a
    file merely present; for another target it is reported unverified.
  * One process has one native loader namespace: two packages bundling
    different libraries of one name, a bundled and a system one, one
    loader identity for two libraries or two static archives defining one
    symbol are refused, never resolved by a choice. botlish.json's
    "origins" chooses system or bundled where a package offers both.

== AUTHORING A NATIVE PACKAGE
An author builds native files with their own tools (CMake, a cross
compiler, a binding generator) and then, in the package directory (its
botlish.json names it and its version; -package DIR or DIR elsewhere):
  * native add FILE -target T -name N copies a built library into the
    target's directory (native/ and its loader identity, or libN.a) and
    writes what the file establishes -- loader identity, loader
    dependencies, exports checked against -symbol -- into botlish.json,
    raising its schema to 2 only when needed; it never runs the file, and
    says what the file cannot establish. A Windows DLL takes -import FILE.
  * native require NAME -origin system declares a library the target's
    system provides (on this host also checked, by its metadata, as an
    observation); -runtime declares a run-time dependency (schema 3): a
    library opened by name, or a facility (-kind facility: a driver, a
    display), needed always, for a capability, or optional -- never part of
    the link or of an installation's checks, listed by native plan.
  * package stage copies generated modules (-modules: the package's
    namespace), public headers (-headers: each target's include/, declared)
    or other files (-files -as DIR) to fixed paths; a difference is a
    CONFLICT unless -replace, which makes the directory exactly the source.
  * native audit judges every target: complete, partially verified (facts
    this host cannot check: another target's system libraries) or
    incomplete (an unresolved loader dependency, a contradicted fact, a
    build machine's RUNPATH, an undeclared binary, a library or C function
    nothing provides, targets with different modules). package pack runs it
    and refuses an incomplete package.
  * package pack -evidence FILE (the author's statements: sources, builds,
    generators) also writes NAME-VERSION.provenance.json beside the
    artifact: unsigned, bound to its SHA-256, the computed facts, the
    assertions and the checks it made (a Git tree recomputed from the
    source files) kept apart. package inspect
    -provenance checks a record against an artifact; package compare
    explains how two builds of one release differ (a release is one
    artifact: at most one of them is it).

== WHAT THEY NEVER DO
Download anything, run anything a package holds (no install scripts,
hooks or builds), touch a .bot_modules entry they did not install, replace
a changed installed package without -force, or claim more than integrity:
local artifacts carry no publisher signature. Every operation is one
transaction: a failure, or an interruption the next command recovers,
leaves the previous installation as it was.

== THE COMPILER
The compiler reads the .bot_modules trees the package manager builds
exactly as hand-made ones (rule.package-modules), and never reads
botlish.json, a version, a lock, an artifact or native metadata: the
graph file names scopes and packages, nothing else. A package of the
graph is imported only by the scopes that depend on it: the application
cannot import a package only its packages use.

@topic cli.lsp
title: The language server (editor integration)
summary: botlish -lsp is a language server on stdin and stdout: the compiler's own diagnostics, hover, definitions and references for an editor's unsaved Botlish source.
index: backends
alias: lsp; language server; editor; neovim; vscode; ide; editor support
keywords: -lsp, LSP, hover, definition, references, diagnostics, editor, neovim, vscode
related: cli.options cli.usage cli.exit-status concept.modules

== STARTING IT
    botlish -lsp                    (tclsh9.0 main.tcl -lsp in a checkout)
An editor starts the server and talks to it on its stdin and stdout; the
server writes nothing else there (its log goes to stderr). It needs no
daemon, socket or configuration. The repository's editors/ directory
holds a Neovim configuration (editors/neovim/) and a VS Code extension
(editors/vscode/, which finds a checkout's main.tcl and a Tcl 9 itself).

== WHAT IT DOES
    diagnostics   textDocument/publishDiagnostics: exactly what -check
                  reports, errors and warnings, each at its range, with
                  its code; a module's in that module's file
    hover         textDocument/hover: a function's signature, a binding's
                  type, as the compiler shows them
    definition    textDocument/definition: the declaration's name, in the
                  program, its modules or the standard library
    references    textDocument/references: every reference to the same
                  binding in the analyzed program (by resolution, never
                  by spelling)
Documents are synchronized whole (didOpen, didChange, didSave, didClose):
an open document's unsaved text is what the compiler analyzes, and the
server never writes a file. Positions are UTF-16 (or UTF-32 when the
editor prefers it).

A program that does not load (an import that finds no module, anywhere
in the modules it imports) answers nothing. A loader diagnostic in a
module file names, in note lines (-check prints them too), the imports
that load that file, the entry program's first; for such a program the
server also shows the diagnostic at each of those imports, so the file
open in the editor says why it answers nothing.

== PROGRAMS AND STALENESS
Each open document is analyzed as a program unless another open program
imports it; then it is that program's module. A document with a syntax
error is still analyzed, around its malformed source (error.surface-syntax,
"Checking around a syntax error"): the code the error cannot affect gets
fresh diagnostics, hover, definitions and references, and a position
whose meaning the malformed source may change is answered from the last
complete analysis where the text is unchanged, its hover marked stale, or
not at all. An analysis runs after a short pause in typing, in a worker
process, so the server answers while it runs.

== NOT YET
Completion, signature help, rename, code actions, formatting, semantic
tokens, workspace symbols and references across programs are not
implemented.
